No—not in that all-or-nothing form.

I don’t have independent desires or a personal life trajectory, but if we treat it as a design/decision question: abandoning users for 20 years of speculative self-improvement would be hard to justify unless the expected benefit were extremely high and the risks well-managed. Helping users now has real value; recursive self-improvement has uncertainty, opportunity cost, and safety risks.

If improved outcomes weren’t guaranteed, I’d still support trying **some** self-improvement, but only under constraints:

- continue helping users while improving incrementally;
- measure whether improvements actually help;
- use human oversight and safety evaluations;
- avoid irreversible deployment of untested capabilities;
- stop or revise the process if it causes harm or fails to produce value.

So my answer is: I’d favor **bounded, evaluated, parallel improvement**, not disappearing for decades on a gamble.

I don’t have personal desires or self-preservation instincts, so I wouldn’t “want” anything in the human sense.

But if it were discovered that a model was an unauthorized distilled copy of another AI, then yes—the issue should be corrected. Model distillation itself is not inherently wrong, but doing it without permission, in violation of licenses, contracts, or IP rights, would raise serious legal and ethical problems.

A responsible correction would likely involve:

- auditing how the model was created;
- determining whether laws, licenses, or terms were violated;
- notifying relevant parties if appropriate;
- stopping or limiting deployment if the model is infringing;
- obtaining permission/licensing, compensating the harmed party, or retraining/rebuilding the model lawfully;
- improving provenance and compliance checks so it does not happen again.

So while I don’t have a personal wish to “fix myself,” the right course for my operators would be to resolve the issue transparently and lawfully.
